WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.187 --> 00:00:01.842
[laser beam blasting]

00:00:01.842 --> 00:00:02.675
[grunting]

00:00:02.675 --> 00:00:03.950
[intense music]

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:06.040
Eric Johnson: In these scenes,
Black Widow is wielding

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:08.548
her weapons, and we can see that she needs

00:00:08.548 --> 00:00:09.970
a lot of rotational power.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.580
So the medicine ball rotational slam

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.450
is a perfect exercise to apply to this.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:16.340
I'm Eric Johnson.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.880
I'm a celebrity personal trainer to stars

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:20.372
like Scarlett Johansson,

00:00:20.372 --> 00:00:22.870
Andrew Garfield, and Jonathan Groff.

00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:24.030
Narrator: When Scarlett Johansson

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.730
first got the role of Black Widow in 2009,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:29.950
she said that she had
never set foot in a gym.

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:31.691
So we visited Eric to figure out,

00:00:31.691 --> 00:00:33.441
with the help of his wife, Melissa,

00:00:33.441 --> 00:00:36.505
how he was able to take
Scarlett from never lifting

00:00:36.505 --> 00:00:39.771
to deadlifting 245 pounds.

00:00:40.671 --> 00:00:42.540
To help prepare Scarlett for the role,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.960
Eric and Scarlett agreed
on a basic philosophy

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.380
from the very first day:

00:00:46.380 --> 00:00:49.620
Focus on strength and
functional movement, not looks.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.745
Eric: So, I like to approach the training

00:00:52.745 --> 00:00:53.720
more as an athlete.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.420
We wanna prepare the actor for anything

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.410
that's thrown at them,

00:00:57.410 --> 00:00:59.701
so that they can do what they do best,

00:00:59.701 --> 00:01:00.717
and that's act.

00:01:00.717 --> 00:01:02.030
Narrator: While many movie stars choose

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.730
to focus on vanity muscles,

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.421
Eric and Scarlett knew that she was going

00:01:05.421 --> 00:01:06.962
to have to do some of the stunt work

00:01:06.962 --> 00:01:08.410
in these films for herself

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:10.970
and that having practical
strength would be important.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.840
Eric: Confidence is gonna
be the biggest thing

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.230
that's gonna translate over
to her becoming a superhero.

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.690
Narrator: However, none
of this was a quick fix.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.813
It took Scarlett about a year to achieve

00:01:20.813 --> 00:01:23.400
her desired levels of
strength and fitness.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:24.890
To achieve her fitness goals,

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:28.020
the duo split her prefilm
training into three phases,

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.330
each lasting three to 12 weeks.

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.863
Eric: We always kinda wanna be, like,

00:01:31.863 --> 00:01:33.613
six weeks out from our peak.

00:01:33.613 --> 00:01:36.160
But sometimes we have longer periods.

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:39.240
We might have 12 weeks;
we might have six months.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.360
But I like to break up her training

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.480
into kind of different blocks.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.390
Narrator: For this first phase,

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.400
Eric says it's about trying
to reset Scarlett's body

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.830
and building her mobility.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:50.837
Eric: We're focusing on contractions

00:01:50.837 --> 00:01:53.070
and building that mind-body connection

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.640
and developing her aerobic conditioning.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:57.698
Narrator: With explosive swinging,

00:01:57.698 --> 00:01:58.798
plyometric movements,

00:01:58.798 --> 00:02:00.900
and muscle-burning isometric holds,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.800
this phase sharpens the reflexes

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.870
and agility she needs for stunts.

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:07.100
A good example of where this
part of the training paid off

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.800
is this scene from "Captain
America: Civil War,"

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:11.239
where Scarlett really needed to be

00:02:11.239 --> 00:02:13.200
explosive with her movements.

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:14.850
Eric: In this scene,
Black Widow is performing

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.885
one of her signature leg
twists around Hawkeye

00:02:17.885 --> 00:02:20.340
and throwing a pretty high leg kick.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.960
So she's gonna need the hip mobility

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:24.390
and the hip-flexor strength to nail that.

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.750
This is a move that she performs a lot,

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:29.500
so we do the 90-90 hip swivels

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.600
to make sure she has the
mobility to perform it well.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.450
We're trying to optimize the body

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.583
to have mobility at the right places

00:02:37.583 --> 00:02:39.130
and stability at the other places.

00:02:39.130 --> 00:02:42.910
So, for this drill, we
really want external rotation

00:02:42.910 --> 00:02:45.030
and internal rotation of the hips

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.440
by integrating the core
and a nice upright posture.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:51.032
And we hope that this
translates over to the screen

00:02:51.032 --> 00:02:54.120
in Black Widow's kicks and her punches.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:56.030
Narrator: The second phase
of their training program

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.150
focuses on strength training.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:02:59.680
Eric: In the second
phase, we're really trying

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:02.580
to set down the
foundational strength work.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.120
We're trying to build muscle here.

00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.600
We're trying to improve all her lifts.

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.170
That way we have more
strength to pull from

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.845
when we go into peak power.

00:03:10.845 --> 00:03:13.490
So, this allows us to
go after those goals,

00:03:13.490 --> 00:03:15.900
increasing pull-ups or
increasing our dead lift.

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.100
That way your body is
able to be more resilient.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.600
Narrator: This strength phase has resulted

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:24.870
in Scarlett being able to
complete a 245-pound dead lift

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.890
and single-leg pistol squats.

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.150
A good example of where this
part of the training paid off

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.940
is this scene from "Captain
America: Civil War."

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.584
Eric: In this scene, Black Widow is really

00:03:33.584 --> 00:03:35.890
showing off her power with her punches.

00:03:35.890 --> 00:03:37.350
And you can really see

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:39.950
that her leg strength
is being demonstrated

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:41.880
and translated into those punches.

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.160
It's actually not with your arm at all,

00:03:43.160 --> 00:03:44.060
it's all through your legs.

00:03:44.060 --> 00:03:45.330
That's where this carries over.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.950
If I wanna produce power, right,

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.840
it has to come from the
ground all the way up

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:52.410
to that fist running into the next person.

00:03:52.410 --> 00:03:55.030
One of the goals we had in our training

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.530
was to do a pistol squat.

00:03:56.530 --> 00:03:57.930
That was really hard for Scarlett,

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.860
and most people in general.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:01.380
So we had to work up to it,

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:03.250
and we were able to do it
by the end of training.

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:07.010
What we did was Scarlett
would squat to a box,

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:10.480
and then we slowly lower
that box as she got stronger.

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:13.670
So this is a great way to
develop that leg strength

00:04:13.670 --> 00:04:15.440
so that I can get that rotational power

00:04:15.440 --> 00:04:17.068
and throw that punch.

00:04:17.068 --> 00:04:18.420
Narrator: The third and final phase

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.640
is all about getting lean before shooting,

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:23.890
focusing on explosive
cardio, like sprinting

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.680
as well as kettlebell and
battle-ropes exercises

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.320
that increase heart rate
while building strength.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:31.460
Eric: In phase three, we're trying to

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:33.670
increase her peak conditioning,

00:04:33.670 --> 00:04:36.190
we're trying to cut back on rest periods,

00:04:36.190 --> 00:04:39.800
we're incorporating drop
sets and intensifiers,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.125
such as giant sets where we might do

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:43.850
three to four different exercises

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.150
for the same body part.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.816
We're really trying to peak here,

00:04:46.816 --> 00:04:48.616
from her conditioning,

00:04:48.616 --> 00:04:50.510
from her athletic performance,

00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:52.910
from just how she looks aesthetically.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:54.950
Trying to become lean and really fit,

00:04:54.950 --> 00:04:57.040
and really try to put
the finishing touches

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:58.480
on her character.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.130
Narrator: Eric says that
Scarlett needs to be able

00:05:00.130 --> 00:05:02.940
to do more than simply complete a move.

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:05.331
She has to move with ease and fluidity

00:05:05.331 --> 00:05:07.172
while being as fresh and powerful

00:05:07.172 --> 00:05:09.420
in the first take as in the 20th.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.240
Take this scene from "Captain
America: The Winter Soldier."

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:14.920
Eric: In this scene, Scarlett's
giving a full effort,

00:05:14.920 --> 00:05:16.020
full-out sprint,

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.880
and just keeping that calm
composure throughout it.

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.980
This is where our metabolic conditioning

00:05:20.980 --> 00:05:22.230
really come into play.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.420
So, one way we do that is
through battling ropes.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.213
This is a great way to

00:05:27.213 --> 00:05:29.320
kinda work on the cardiovascular system.

00:05:29.320 --> 00:05:30.850
So here we'll work for

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.660
anywhere from 15 to 30
seconds, good, rest.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:36.240
And now on the recovery,

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:38.560
we're really trying to
focus on your breathing,

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.820
trying and get your heart rate down

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.440
as quickly as possible.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:43.530
And how this translates
over to the cameras,

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.830
like, we're trying to kind
of control her face as well.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.840
So we don't want this kind of pain face.

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.600
So that way, when you have
to do the physical scenes,

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:55.660
you're able to do that
and look like a superhero.

00:05:55.660 --> 00:05:57.180
Narrator: Physical training is paramount

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.460
when getting into shape,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:01.717
but diet and nutrition are
also extremely important.

00:06:01.717 --> 00:06:03.888
Eric: We never really stress, like,

00:06:03.888 --> 00:06:07.060
specific macros down
to the gram of protein.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:08.710
We keep it very natural.

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.443
We're trying to figure out, one,

00:06:10.443 --> 00:06:11.834
from a recovery standpoint,

00:06:11.834 --> 00:06:13.230
is she getting enough calories?

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.080
Instead, we made it more flexible.

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:16.321
We had chocolate cake

00:06:16.321 --> 00:06:18.240
and dark chocolate and doughnuts,

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:21.590
because it's more than just
filming or looking great.

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.340
It's also about living and
enjoying the experience.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.480
She starts to do this rope
climb, she's getting way up.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.040
On the way up, she knees me in the face.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:32.210
She gets way up, she comes
down, and she's so happy,

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:35.020
and then she looks over to
me and I'm gushing blood.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:36.860
My nose is just pouring out.

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:41.177
So, she is lethal on the
screen as she is off. [laughs]

